From iaingallagher at btopenworld.com  Thu Mar 31 17:31:55 2011
From: iaingallagher at btopenworld.com (Iain Gallagher)
Date: Thu, 31 Mar 2011 16:31:55 +0100
Subject: [BioC] GoStats and microRNA pipeline using Biomart
Message-ID: <614859.80170.qm@web86704.mail.ird.yahoo.com>

Hi David

I'm not sure that you need to / should normalise based on UTR length.

The mechanism of miRNA target repression is mostly thought to results from miRNA binding to the 3'UTR and guiding the target mRNA to an AGO containing processing unit for destruction. This may not be the only mechanism. In any case the targeting of a mRNA by a miRNA is dependent on complementarity between the seed region of the miRNA (bases 2-7 or 8) and the the 3'UTR. There's also some evidence that complementarity at bases 12/13 - 16/17 of the miRNA are also important in binding. Furthermore the position of the binding site in the UTR is also important (see the Bartel groups TargetScan papers for more info here - Friedman et al I think and Grimson et al) so some binding sites may be spurious.

Thus the targeting of a given mRNA by a miRNA is not really a function of UTR length per se but UTR sequence. Presumably the algorithm you have used to select miRNA targets takes into account the sequence (i.e. you know that there is complementarity already). 

From your original email you want to correct for the expectation that a given miRNA may target the UTR multiple times when you analyse for GO categories. Would it not be better to weight multiply targeted genes so that they contribute more to the enrichment analysis. So how would one go about this?

Perhaps you need to generate the list of genes that are targeted by miRNA-A, examine this list (using e.g. GOstats) for enriched GO categories, pull out the genes associated with each category (geneIdsByCategory in the Category lib), examine the categories for genes which are multiply targeted and then weight the p-value for a given GO term appropriately (perhaps a simple multiplication by the number of times genes are targeted) and re-rank the p-values.

Bear in mind though that just because a UTR is targeted multiple times by a given miRNA (and that predictions differ widely across algorithms still) that particular mRNA may be no more 'repressed' than if it was targeted once.

Anyway - my tuppence worth!

Hope it's helpful to you

best

i

